Hello, Sherry.

263
00:16:12,004 --> 00:16:13,996
Looking pretty fine
for a dead woman.

264
00:16:15,041 --> 00:16:17,119
Some mail came for you.

265
00:16:17,143 --> 00:16:18,454
What is he doing here?

266
00:16:18,478 --> 00:16:20,456
Name's McMasters.

267
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:23,006
- He's a new dealer.
- No, he's a private investigator.

268
00:16:27,687 --> 00:16:29,331
Hey!

269
00:16:29,355 --> 00:16:30,799
All right, hey!

270
00:16:30,823 --> 00:16:33,001
You don't have to get ugly.

271
00:16:33,025 --> 00:16:34,236
I just came to find Sherry.

272
00:16:34,260 --> 00:16:35,871
Now that I found
her, I'll just leave.

273
00:16:35,895 --> 00:16:36,872
- Sit down!
- Hey!

274
00:16:36,896 --> 00:16:39,174
And head straight
for the police.

275
00:16:39,198 --> 00:16:40,909
I got no reason to do that.

276
00:16:40,933 --> 00:16:43,011
You lie a lot, you
know that, Conrad?

277
00:16:43,035 --> 00:16:45,347
Hey, if you're so worried
about the cops, you should do

278
00:16:45,371 --> 00:16:46,515
something about her, not me.

279
00:16:46,539 --> 00:16:47,683
What does that mean?

280
00:16:47,707 --> 00:16:49,551
It means, as soon
as the police in Atlanta

281
00:16:49,575 --> 00:16:51,186
find out she cleared
out her bank account,

282
00:16:51,210 --> 00:16:53,338
they're gonna be
on your doorstep.

283
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:56,024
Or didn't she tell you?

284
00:16:56,048 --> 00:16:57,993
He's out of his mind.

285
00:16:58,017 --> 00:16:59,261
Tell me what?

286
00:16:59,285 --> 00:17:00,796
Tell you that

287
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:03,365
Sherry's gotten herself into
so much trouble in Atlanta

288
00:17:03,389 --> 00:17:06,168
that she faked her own
death to get everybody...

289
00:17:06,192 --> 00:17:08,370
Including the
police... Off her back.

290
00:17:08,394 --> 00:17:10,172
Is that true?

291
00:17:10,196 --> 00:17:11,630
No.

292
00:17:12,698 --> 00:17:14,309
Hey, lightning's
gonna strike you.

293
00:17:14,333 --> 00:17:15,944
- Shut up!
- I said I'd help you

294
00:17:15,968 --> 00:17:17,679
because Eric and
I were old friends.

295
00:17:17,703 --> 00:17:19,515
- Now, did you lie to me, honey?
- No.

296
00:17:19,539 --> 00:17:20,516
No.

297
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:21,917
He's lying.

298
00:17:21,941 --> 00:17:23,252
Kick him out of here, Don.

299
00:17:23,276 --> 00:17:24,820
Yeah, well, one of you is lying.

300
00:17:24,844 --> 00:17:27,871
And being around liars
makes me uncomfortable!

301
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:35,330
Thanks a lot. Now I
have no place to go.

302
00:17:35,354 --> 00:17:36,932
I'm just trying to find
out who killed Eric.

303
00:17:36,956 --> 00:17:39,034
Yeah, well, I didn't, so why
don't you just leave me alone?

304
00:17:39,058 --> 00:17:42,051
Sherry! You're the only
one that can help me.

305
00:18:03,249 --> 00:18:05,946
♪♪

306
00:18:19,765 --> 00:18:21,543
Sherry.

307
00:18:21,567 --> 00:18:24,146
Look, look... the
murderer's got to be one of

308
00:18:24,170 --> 00:18:25,581
the three guys you've
been blackmailing.

309
00:18:25,605 --> 00:18:27,382
- Aren't you the genius!
- Hey!

310
00:18:27,406 --> 00:18:29,217
You are playing a
very dangerous game.

311
00:18:29,241 --> 00:18:31,486
No more. Game's over.
I've gone into retirement.

312
00:18:31,510 --> 00:18:32,654
Think! Think.

313
00:18:32,678 --> 00:18:34,356
They're not gonna
let you off the hook

314
00:18:34,380 --> 00:18:36,491
until they get the
negatives and you're dead.

315
00:18:36,515 --> 00:18:37,559
I'm telling the truth.

316
00:18:37,583 --> 00:18:39,394
They're gonna find
you, just like I did.

317
00:18:39,418 --> 00:18:41,944
And when they do, they're
gonna take their time killing you.

318
00:18:50,096 --> 00:18:52,641
The negatives are in
a locker in the airport.

319
00:18:52,665 --> 00:18:54,176
"C" Concourse.

320
00:18:54,200 --> 00:18:56,260
What... the negatives...
What are they of?

321
00:18:58,270 --> 00:19:00,262
You'll figure it out.

322
00:19:17,490 --> 00:19:19,101
Oh, my God!

323
00:19:25,197 --> 00:19:26,341
Call the police.

324
00:19:26,365 --> 00:19:27,843
Please, nobody go outside.

325
00:19:27,867 --> 00:19:28,844
What happened?

326
00:19:28,868 --> 00:19:30,359
Somebody jumped.

327
00:19:56,162 --> 00:19:59,274
<i>I probably led
them</i> right to her.

328
00:19:59,298 --> 00:20:02,711
I should've just walked
her to her room, huh?

329
00:20:02,735 --> 00:20:05,380
Oh, she knew what she was into.

330
00:20:05,404 --> 00:20:07,315
She knew it got Eric killed.

331
00:20:07,339 --> 00:20:08,784
That didn't stop her.

332
00:20:08,808 --> 00:20:10,318
You couldn't have done anything.

333
00:20:10,342 --> 00:20:12,988
But I was right there.

334
00:20:13,012 --> 00:20:14,322
So was the killer.

335
00:20:14,346 --> 00:20:17,693
He ought to be
feeling bad, not you.

336
00:20:17,717 --> 00:20:20,162
Let's see here, now.

337
00:20:20,186 --> 00:20:21,814
Hmm.

338
00:20:22,988 --> 00:20:24,399
All right.

339
00:20:24,423 --> 00:20:28,236
John Delaney, Tom Hermanski,
Roy Stevens... three suspects.

340
00:20:29,261 --> 00:20:30,605
- Little younger, though.
- Yeah.

341
00:20:30,629 --> 00:20:33,041
Judging from the car, I'd
say about 15 years younger.

342
00:20:33,065 --> 00:20:34,042
Wouldn't you?

343
00:20:34,066 --> 00:20:35,477
Yeah, that would
put them in college.

344
00:20:35,501 --> 00:20:36,778
Yeah.

345
00:20:36,802 --> 00:20:39,135
What do they got in
their hands, paintings?

346
00:20:41,140 --> 00:20:43,118
Looks like it. What
would a bunch of jocks

347
00:20:43,142 --> 00:20:44,686
want with a bunch of paintings?

348
00:20:44,710 --> 00:20:46,388
I don't know.

349
00:20:46,412 --> 00:20:50,358
You don't suppose they paid
Eric Gaston all that money

350
00:20:50,382 --> 00:20:54,820
just to keep, you know,
that a secret, do you?

351
00:20:56,856 --> 00:20:59,334
You know, if memory serves me,

352
00:20:59,358 --> 00:21:04,906
I would say this is
the... untitled Braque.

353
00:21:04,930 --> 00:21:06,575
And I would say this one is

354
00:21:06,599 --> 00:21:09,044
the early Picasso.

355
00:21:09,068 --> 00:21:10,212
They're valuable?

356
00:21:10,236 --> 00:21:14,449
Well... that depends.

357
00:21:14,473 --> 00:21:17,219
On what?

358
00:21:17,243 --> 00:21:20,222
On how well the buyer
of the stolen paintings

359
00:21:20,246 --> 00:21:21,223
took care of them.

360
00:21:21,247 --> 00:21:22,457
I knew it.

361
00:21:22,481 --> 00:21:25,627
Okay, when were they
stolen and where from?

362
00:21:25,651 --> 00:21:28,730
The Metropolitan
Gallery in Atlanta.

363
00:21:28,754 --> 00:21:31,433
About 15 years ago.

364
00:21:31,457 --> 00:21:33,468
Unless I'm mistaken.

365
00:21:33,492 --> 00:21:36,705
But then, you see, I rarely am.

366
00:21:36,729 --> 00:21:37,906
Right, right.

367
00:21:37,930 --> 00:21:38,907
Were the thieves caught?

368
00:21:38,931 --> 00:21:40,041
No.

369
00:21:40,065 --> 00:21:42,944
But something else
happened that night.

370
00:21:42,968 --> 00:21:44,713
What happened?

371
00:21:44,737 --> 00:21:48,116
A security guard
was shot and killed.

372
00:21:48,140 --> 00:21:50,719
This meant it wasn't
only a burglary;

373
00:21:50,743 --> 00:21:53,076
it was murder.

374
00:21:56,916 --> 00:21:59,227
Now we know why Eric
Gaston was able to blackmail

375
00:21:59,251 --> 00:22:00,929
those three guys for so long.

376
00:22:00,953 --> 00:22:01,997
Yeah.

377
00:22:02,021 --> 00:22:03,465
Murder, that makes a difference.

378
00:22:03,489 --> 00:22:05,934
If it'd been just
simple robbery,

379
00:22:05,958 --> 00:22:08,069
those negatives
would be worthless.

380
00:22:08,093 --> 00:22:09,538
Why?

381
00:22:09,562 --> 00:22:10,639
Oh, yeah, yeah.

382
00:22:10,663 --> 00:22:12,007
- Statute of limitations.
- Yeah.

383
00:22:12,031 --> 00:22:14,557
Run out a long time ago.

384
00:22:15,701 --> 00:22:18,046
Wonder which one of
them pulled the trigger.

385
00:22:18,070 --> 00:22:20,849
Well, they could
have hired somebody.

386
00:22:20,873 --> 00:22:22,717
Oh, I don't think so.

387
00:22:22,741 --> 00:22:25,587
It feels like one of
them, don't you think?

388
00:22:25,611 --> 00:22:27,656
Yeah, yeah.

389
00:22:27,680 --> 00:22:30,325
Why an art gallery?

390
00:22:30,349 --> 00:22:32,594
Yeah, why not a bank

391
00:22:32,618 --> 00:22:34,496
or a sporting goods
store or a liquor store?

392
00:22:34,520 --> 00:22:38,834
Yeah. Three football
players robbing an art gallery.

393
00:22:38,858 --> 00:22:40,554
Strange, isn't it?

394
00:22:44,330 --> 00:22:45,307
<i>Art majors?</i>

395
00:22:45,331 --> 00:22:46,308
Are you kidding?

396
00:22:46,332 --> 00:22:47,876
Hasn't been an art major

397
00:22:47,900 --> 00:22:51,713
on a Baxter University
football team since 1968.

398
00:22:51,737 --> 00:22:53,081
George Stolberg.

399
00:22:53,105 --> 00:22:55,684
Yeah, he thought it was going
to help him avoid the draft.

400
00:22:55,708 --> 00:22:57,752
Well, maybe one of
them was an art minor.

401
00:22:57,776 --> 00:22:59,955
No, not a chance.
Not those three.

402
00:22:59,979 --> 00:23:03,692
The closest they ever got to art
was the Sunday morning funnies.

403
00:23:03,716 --> 00:23:04,993
What about part-time jobs?

404
00:23:05,017 --> 00:23:08,263
Well, Stevens, uh,
worked the radio station,

405
00:23:08,287 --> 00:23:13,301
and it, uh, seems to me that,
uh, Delaney worked tables

406
00:23:13,325 --> 00:23:14,603
at one of the sorority houses.

407
00:23:14,627 --> 00:23:15,837
Tom Hermanski?

408
00:23:15,861 --> 00:23:17,706
Tom, yeah, he, uh...

409
00:23:17,730 --> 00:23:20,256
made a career out
of dating rich girls.

410
00:23:21,934 --> 00:23:25,547
You know, I think
one of those guys

411
00:23:25,571 --> 00:23:27,506
used to go with an art major.

412
00:23:29,074 --> 00:23:32,254
Yeah, her daddy owned
an art gallery or something.

413
00:23:32,278 --> 00:23:34,623
Big mucky-muck at some museum.

414
00:23:34,647 --> 00:23:36,424
Who dated her? You remember?

415
00:23:36,448 --> 00:23:39,816
Well, now let me
think about that.

416
00:24:00,873 --> 00:24:03,604
♪ It's time to make a change ♪

417
00:24:06,045 --> 00:24:09,090
♪ Time to pick myself up ♪

418
00:24:09,114 --> 00:24:12,209
♪ Get up off the floor ♪

419
00:24:15,955 --> 00:24:18,733
♪ 'Cause I've been there, baby ♪

420
00:24:18,757 --> 00:24:21,670
♪ I've felt all the pain I can ♪

421
00:24:21,694 --> 00:24:26,574
♪ No more till the blues
come back knocking ♪

422
00:24:26,598 --> 00:24:31,002
♪ At my door ♪

423
00:24:32,004 --> 00:24:35,216
♪ Mm, mm, mm... ♪

424
00:24:35,240 --> 00:24:37,675
♪ My door. ♪

425
00:24:51,023 --> 00:24:53,735
How's it going, Ben?

426
00:24:53,759 --> 00:24:56,888
Pretty good, Ty. How about you?

427
00:24:58,597 --> 00:25:00,862
So far, okay.

428
00:25:13,512 --> 00:25:15,790
Have you any idea
what we're looking for?

429
00:25:15,814 --> 00:25:19,294
Anything that'll tell us
who killed Eric Gaston.

430
00:25:19,318 --> 00:25:20,662
You know, none of our suspects

431
00:25:20,686 --> 00:25:22,564
are in any of these
team pictures.

432
00:25:22,588 --> 00:25:24,833
Well, they didn't make the
team till Eric was a senior.

433
00:25:24,857 --> 00:25:28,003
Remember, that was
the year he got expelled.

434
00:25:28,027 --> 00:25:30,505
He must not have ordered
a picture, then, that year.

435
00:25:30,529 --> 00:25:32,340
Well, he probably
wasn't in any of them.

436
00:25:32,364 --> 00:25:33,908
He only played half a year.

437
00:25:33,932 --> 00:25:36,578
I bet you that made him mad,
getting kicked out like that.

438
00:25:36,602 --> 00:25:38,880
Not being able to enjoy
all that stuff that goes along

439
00:25:38,904 --> 00:25:41,066
with being in a
championship team.

440
00:25:43,475 --> 00:25:44,943
What?

441
00:25:58,223 --> 00:26:00,668
<i>Well, Mr. Delaney,
how's business?</i>

442
00:26:00,692 --> 00:26:02,037
Just fine.

443
00:26:02,061 --> 00:26:04,639
Your secretary, Mrs. Harrison,

444
00:26:04,663 --> 00:26:06,408
showed me your client list

445
00:26:06,432 --> 00:26:08,610
for each of the last five years,

446
00:26:08,634 --> 00:26:12,380
and, uh, you know,
it's getting smaller.

447
00:26:12,404 --> 00:26:13,748
Well, it's only because

448
00:26:13,772 --> 00:26:15,850
my clients' salaries
keep getting bigger.

449
00:26:15,874 --> 00:26:17,152
Yeah.

450
00:26:17,176 --> 00:26:18,753
Yeah, and-and-and she told me

451
00:26:18,777 --> 00:26:20,688
that you recently
sold your house

452
00:26:20,712 --> 00:26:22,190
and moved into an apartment

453
00:26:22,214 --> 00:26:25,393
and that you sold your
Porsche that you used to drive

454
00:26:25,417 --> 00:26:28,263
and that you did
have two secretaries,

455
00:26:28,287 --> 00:26:31,199
but about a month ago
you let the other one go.

456
00:26:31,223 --> 00:26:34,769
That sounds like considerable
belt-tightening to me.

457
00:26:34,793 --> 00:26:37,472
Well, I prefer to
call it "streamlining."

458
00:26:37,496 --> 00:26:39,007
Yeah.

459
00:26:39,031 --> 00:26:41,709
The last thing you
needed was for Eric Gaston

460
00:26:41,733 --> 00:26:43,845
to blackmail you for
more money, wasn't it?

461
00:26:43,869 --> 00:26:46,681
Never been
blackmailed, Mr. Matlock.

462
00:26:46,705 --> 00:26:48,817
Not by Eric, not by anybody.

463
00:26:48,841 --> 00:26:53,588
Are you going to deny that
that's you in that picture?

464
00:26:53,612 --> 00:26:55,723
All I know is, I couldn't
possibly have taken part

465
00:26:55,747 --> 00:26:56,791
in that art theft.

466
00:26:56,815 --> 00:26:58,359
Why not?

467
00:26:58,383 --> 00:27:00,762
Well, I did a little
checking, Mr. Matlock.

468
00:27:00,786 --> 00:27:02,430
Seems to me that
robbery took place

469
00:27:02,454 --> 00:27:05,100
late on a Saturday afternoon
in November, is that right?

470
00:27:05,124 --> 00:27:07,068
That's right. It was...
Let's see now, hold it...

471
00:27:07,092 --> 00:27:10,839
It was November 11, 1975,
between 2:30 and 3:00.

472
00:27:10,863 --> 00:27:13,508
Sorry. Couldn't
have been involved.

473
00:27:13,532 --> 00:27:14,909
Why?

474
00:27:14,933 --> 00:27:16,778
Well, I was doing
the same thing I did

475
00:27:16,802 --> 00:27:20,348
every other Saturday afternoon
back then... playing football

476
00:27:20,372 --> 00:27:23,570
in front of several thousand
Baxter University fans.

477
00:27:25,310 --> 00:27:27,589
Nice try, but no cigar.

478
00:27:27,613 --> 00:27:30,291
You remember, uh, um,

479
00:27:30,315 --> 00:27:32,093
yeah, Martin Kravetz...
Remember him?

480
00:27:32,117 --> 00:27:34,762
Trainer over there at Baxter.

481
00:27:34,786 --> 00:27:37,565
Biggest football
fan I've ever seen.

482
00:27:37,589 --> 00:27:41,603
You ask him the score
of any Baxter game,

483
00:27:41,627 --> 00:27:44,839
and no... no matter how
long ago, he'll give it to you.

484
00:27:44,863 --> 00:27:46,941
And if he's not sure,
he'll get out his scrapbook

485
00:27:46,965 --> 00:27:50,311
of newspaper
clippings and look it up.

486
00:27:50,335 --> 00:27:52,280
See?

487
00:27:52,304 --> 00:27:54,716
You played football
that afternoon,

488
00:27:54,740 --> 00:27:57,051
but only nine minutes
of the first quarter,

489
00:27:57,075 --> 00:28:00,822
when you and Roy Stevens
were ejected for fighting.

490
00:28:00,846 --> 00:28:03,625
That means you had to
leave the field, didn't you?

491
00:28:03,649 --> 00:28:07,695
And, interestingly enough,
Tom Hermanski hit the showers

492
00:28:07,719 --> 00:28:10,365
with some kind
of shoulder injury.

493
00:28:10,389 --> 00:28:12,767
Now, the Metropolitan Art Museum

494
00:28:12,791 --> 00:28:15,436
is only a ten-minute
drive from the stadium.

495
00:28:15,460 --> 00:28:18,139
You could have gone over
there, used the information

496
00:28:18,163 --> 00:28:20,208
that Tom Hermanski
got from his girlfriend,

497
00:28:20,232 --> 00:28:22,677
helped yourself to a
couple of paintings,

498
00:28:22,701 --> 00:28:26,548
put them in the trunk of the
car and been back to the stadium

499
00:28:26,572 --> 00:28:29,050
by halftime, with
no one the wiser.

500
00:28:29,074 --> 00:28:30,985
And that's exactly
what happened, isn't it?

501
00:28:31,009 --> 00:28:32,887
Of course not.
That's ridiculous.

502
00:28:32,911 --> 00:28:35,156
How did Eric get onto you?

503
00:28:35,180 --> 00:28:38,993
Did-Did he hear you whispering
in the locker room about your...

504
00:28:39,017 --> 00:28:42,530
your plans as to
this famous scam?

505
00:28:42,554 --> 00:28:44,399
I did not steal any paintings,

506
00:28:44,423 --> 00:28:46,654
and Eric was not
blackmailing me.

507
00:28:48,026 --> 00:28:50,505
Okay.

508
00:28:50,529 --> 00:28:54,876
Where were you
between 9:30 and 10:30

509
00:28:54,900 --> 00:28:57,131
the morning Eric was killed?

510
00:28:59,104 --> 00:29:01,015
I was in my office.

511
00:29:01,039 --> 00:29:02,550
I got there at 9:00.

512
00:29:02,574 --> 00:29:04,819
I didn't leave till
almost 7:00 that night.

513
00:29:04,843 --> 00:29:06,287
Can anybody verify that?

514
00:29:06,311 --> 00:29:08,223
Absolutely... my secretary.

515
00:29:08,247 --> 00:29:09,791
No.

516
00:29:09,815 --> 00:29:12,827
No, she was running an
errand for you. Remember?

517
00:29:12,851 --> 00:29:14,562
You called her at her house

518
00:29:14,586 --> 00:29:16,864
at 7:00 that morning
and asked her

519
00:29:16,888 --> 00:29:20,401
to go to a box office, get you
some baseball tickets. Remember?

520
00:29:20,425 --> 00:29:22,203
Oh, I do remember that.

521
00:29:22,227 --> 00:29:24,372
Yeah, she didn't get in till

522
00:29:24,396 --> 00:29:26,274
- almost 10:00.
- 11:00.

523
00:29:26,298 --> 00:29:28,710
And that would have
given you plenty of time

524
00:29:28,734 --> 00:29:30,645
to return to the office

525
00:29:30,669 --> 00:29:32,714
after you'd killed Eric Gaston.

526
00:29:32,738 --> 00:29:34,349
That's not true.

527
00:29:34,373 --> 00:29:37,452
I was in my office that morning,
and you can't prove otherwise.

528
00:29:37,476 --> 00:29:39,387
Oh, I'm afraid I can.

529
00:29:39,411 --> 00:29:43,157
You remember that
big coffee stain on, uh...

530
00:29:43,181 --> 00:29:45,560
the chair in your office?

531
00:29:45,584 --> 00:29:49,230
Well, your secretary
had arranged

532
00:29:49,254 --> 00:29:52,300
with Gold's Cleaning
Service to come over at 9:30

533
00:29:52,324 --> 00:29:54,168
that morning and steam it out.

534
00:29:54,192 --> 00:29:56,604
I guess she forgot to mention
it to you when you called her

535
00:29:56,628 --> 00:29:58,273
at home, 'cause it was so early.

536
00:29:58,297 --> 00:30:02,377
Anyway, when Mr. Gold
got there at 9:35,

537
00:30:02,401 --> 00:30:04,012
he couldn't get in.

538
00:30:04,036 --> 00:30:05,880
Nobody was there.

539
00:30:05,904 --> 00:30:07,248
The lights were out.

540
00:30:07,272 --> 00:30:08,549
The doors were locked.

541
00:30:08,573 --> 00:30:10,785
Here's his invoice,
two days later.

542
00:30:10,809 --> 00:30:11,919
See?

543
00:30:11,943 --> 00:30:13,921
Says, "Unable to render services

544
00:30:13,945 --> 00:30:16,591
at 9:30 a.m. Tuesday the 17th"

545
00:30:16,615 --> 00:30:21,229
because of... "no one was
at the office" to let him in.

546
00:30:22,688 --> 00:30:25,133
Well, he charged
you $30, anyway.

547
00:30:25,157 --> 00:30:28,036
Highway robbery.

548
00:30:28,060 --> 00:30:31,506
Well, now, we know
you were not at...

549
00:30:31,530 --> 00:30:33,841
at your office at the
time of the murder.

550
00:30:33,865 --> 00:30:35,777
You care to tell us
where you were?

551
00:30:40,138 --> 00:30:41,749
No?

552
00:30:41,773 --> 00:30:44,919
Well...

553
00:30:44,943 --> 00:30:49,424
This ring was found on
Eric's finger by the police

554
00:30:49,448 --> 00:30:51,125
when they arrived at the scene.

555
00:30:51,149 --> 00:30:52,947
You recognize it?

556
00:30:54,386 --> 00:30:55,363
No.

557
00:30:55,387 --> 00:30:57,098
You don't?

558
00:30:57,122 --> 00:31:00,968
Well, rings like this were
given to all the Baxter players

559
00:31:00,992 --> 00:31:03,705
in 1975, the year you
won the championship.

560
00:31:03,729 --> 00:31:05,807
See? It says so right there.

561
00:31:05,831 --> 00:31:08,409
I don't know... it's kind of hard
to recognize without the stone.

562
00:31:08,433 --> 00:31:10,144
Yeah, the ring is a mess.

563
00:31:10,168 --> 00:31:15,216
The police think that
maybe... that maybe, uh,

564
00:31:15,240 --> 00:31:19,754
Eric struggled with his
killer, and just before he died,

565
00:31:19,778 --> 00:31:22,423
hit him so hard that
he crushed the stone.

566
00:31:22,447 --> 00:31:25,426
But I have a couple
of problems with that.

567
00:31:25,450 --> 00:31:27,095
I have a couple of problems.

568
00:31:27,119 --> 00:31:30,998
The coroner found
a long, jagged cut

569
00:31:31,022 --> 00:31:33,201
along Eric's left jaw.

570
00:31:33,225 --> 00:31:36,003
And his report
said it was inflicted

571
00:31:36,027 --> 00:31:38,039
just moments before his death.

572
00:31:38,063 --> 00:31:40,508
And that would suggest that Eric

573
00:31:40,532 --> 00:31:44,412
was the one that got
hit, rather than vice versa.

574
00:31:44,436 --> 00:31:47,715
And the other thing is,
Eric didn't have a ring.

575
00:31:47,739 --> 00:31:49,784
Of course he did. He
was a star running back.

576
00:31:49,808 --> 00:31:51,352
- Remember?
- No, no, no, no.

577
00:31:51,376 --> 00:31:53,354
That was the year he
was expelled for gambling.

578
00:31:53,378 --> 00:31:54,422
Remember?

579
00:31:54,446 --> 00:31:55,690
He only played a half season.

580
00:31:55,714 --> 00:31:56,958
He didn't get a ring.

581
00:31:56,982 --> 00:31:58,960
Look at this picture.

582
00:31:58,984 --> 00:32:02,764
Now, this picture was
taken at, uh... at Eric's club

583
00:32:02,788 --> 00:32:04,565
the night before he was killed.

584
00:32:04,589 --> 00:32:05,566
Now, look here.

585
00:32:05,590 --> 00:32:08,269
All of them have rings.

586
00:32:08,293 --> 00:32:09,937
See, they all... all have rings.

587
00:32:09,961 --> 00:32:12,328
But-But Eric... no ring.

588
00:32:16,001 --> 00:32:17,378
See, no ring.

589
00:32:17,402 --> 00:32:18,646
No ring.

590
00:32:18,670 --> 00:32:21,082
What's...

591
00:32:21,106 --> 00:32:24,152
what's happened to
your ring, Mr. Delaney?

592
00:32:24,176 --> 00:32:27,622
I, uh... lost it recently.

593
00:32:27,646 --> 00:32:28,875
Yeah?

594
00:32:30,916 --> 00:32:33,761
Are you sure this
isn't your ring?

595
00:32:33,785 --> 00:32:36,564
You sure you didn't
struggle with Eric

596
00:32:36,588 --> 00:32:38,299
just before you shot him

597
00:32:38,323 --> 00:32:42,036
and hit him so hard that
you crushed the stone

598
00:32:42,060 --> 00:32:44,405
in his ring into
hundreds of pieces?

599
00:32:44,429 --> 00:32:47,341
You knew my client
would be walking

600
00:32:47,365 --> 00:32:49,243
through the door any minute.

601
00:32:49,267 --> 00:32:51,646
You sure you
didn't take that ring

602
00:32:51,670 --> 00:32:53,614
off your finger and
put it on Eric's finger

603
00:32:53,638 --> 00:32:56,350
so everybody would believe
it was his instead of yours?

604
00:32:56,374 --> 00:32:58,052
Look, you can twist
things all you want,

605
00:32:58,076 --> 00:32:59,987
but that is not my ring.

606
00:33:00,011 --> 00:33:01,775
Conrad?

607
00:33:14,125 --> 00:33:15,593
You recognize her?

608
00:33:17,362 --> 00:33:19,092
I don't know.

609
00:33:20,599 --> 00:33:22,343
That's Robin Martel,

610
00:33:22,367 --> 00:33:25,480
the girl you went
with in college.

611
00:33:25,504 --> 00:33:29,217
The girl who wore
your ring for two years.

612
00:33:29,241 --> 00:33:30,885
She'll testify

613
00:33:30,909 --> 00:33:34,641
that she had it cut down
so it would fit her finger.

614
00:33:35,981 --> 00:33:38,960
And when you took it
back two years later,

615
00:33:38,984 --> 00:33:41,095
you had to wear it
on your pinky finger,

616
00:33:41,119 --> 00:33:43,498
because it was too
big for the other fingers.

617
00:33:43,522 --> 00:33:48,135
In other words, she will testify
that this is, indeed, your ring.

618
00:33:48,159 --> 00:33:50,104
And when she does that,

619
00:33:50,128 --> 00:33:52,540
I'm going to enter
it into evidence.

620
00:33:52,564 --> 00:33:55,676
And when I do that,
two things will happen:

621
00:33:55,700 --> 00:33:59,814
We will have proven
beyond a reasonable doubt

622
00:33:59,838 --> 00:34:03,618
that my client over there
did not commit this crime;

623
00:34:03,642 --> 00:34:07,922
and we're going to make
you look awfully suspicious,

624
00:34:07,946 --> 00:34:12,159
not only for what happened
to Eric, but for what happened

625
00:34:12,183 --> 00:34:14,896
to his girlfriend, Sherry
Brown, in Chicago.

626
00:34:14,920 --> 00:34:19,934
And later on down the line,
what happened to that guard

627
00:34:19,958 --> 00:34:22,069
during the art theft.

628
00:34:22,093 --> 00:34:24,739
Mr. Delaney, you
remember once, you told me

629
00:34:24,763 --> 00:34:28,509
"Gambling is for losers."

630
00:34:28,533 --> 00:34:29,777
And it is.

631
00:34:29,801 --> 00:34:32,313
And it seems... it seems
almost trite to say now

632
00:34:32,337 --> 00:34:35,917
that murder is always
a gamble, and...

633
00:34:35,941 --> 00:34:38,753
you took it and... and-and lost.

634
00:34:45,650 --> 00:34:47,295
Ladies and
gentlemen of the jury,

635
00:34:47,319 --> 00:34:48,629
have you reached a verdict?

636
00:34:48,653 --> 00:34:52,066
We find the defendant,
Tyler Mullins,

637
00:34:52,090 --> 00:34:53,834
not guilty. -

638
00:34:53,858 --> 00:34:56,070
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury.

639
00:34:56,094 --> 00:34:58,406
This court is adjourned.

640
00:34:59,431 --> 00:35:01,542
- Well...
- Thank you, Ben.

641
00:35:01,566 --> 00:35:04,278
Thank you, Conrad.

